\layout Subsection*

Steady State Probabilities
\layout Standard

Using the detailed balance equations we find 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
P(k) & = & \frac{\lambda _{k}}{\mu _{k}}P(k-1)\\
 & = & \frac{\lambda }{k\mu }P(k-1)\\
 & = & \frac{a}{k}P(k-1)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset 

 Which means that 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
P(k) & = & \prod _{n=1}^{k}\frac{a}{n}P(0)\\
 & = & \frac{a^{k}}{k!}P(0)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset 

 The normalisation condition gives us 
\begin_inset Formula \[
P(0)\sum _{k=0}^{m}\frac{a^{k}}{k!}=1\]

\end_inset 


\begin_inset Formula \[
P(0)=\frac{1}{\sum _{k=0}^{m}\frac{a^{k}}{k!}}\]

\end_inset 

 which leads to the truncated Poisson distribution 
\begin_inset Formula \[
P(k)=\frac{\frac{a^{k}}{k!}}{\sum _{n=0}^{m}\frac{a^{n}}{n!}}\]

\end_inset 

 Note if 
\begin_inset Formula $m\rightarrow \infty $
\end_inset 

 we recover the 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/\infty $
\end_inset 

 queue as we would expect.
\layout Subsection*

Performance Measures for Erlang B
\layout Standard

For the blocking probability and the Erlang B curves we are interested in
 the probability that when we arrive the queue is full 
\begin_inset Formula \[
B(m,a)=P(m)=\frac{\frac{a^{m}}{m!}}{\sum _{n=0}^{m}\frac{a^{n}}{n!}}\]

\end_inset 

 
