\layout Subsection*

Carried Load
\layout Standard

Since traffic is lost in 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/m/m$
\end_inset 

 the carried load, 
\begin_inset Formula $a^{\prime }$
\end_inset 

, is smaller than the offered load.
 It may be found as 
\begin_inset Formula \[
a^{\prime }=a(1-P(m))=a(1-B(m,a))\]

\end_inset 

 The carried load per line is the trunk occupancy, 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\rho =\frac{a^{\prime }}{m},\]

\end_inset 

 or the utilization factor --- it is a measure of efficiency of the line.
 Note the contrast with the lossless systems where 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\rho =\frac{a}{m}\]

\end_inset 

because 
\begin_inset Formula $a^{\prime }=a$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Example*

Load tripling.
\layout Example*

For a blocking probability of 0.01, what is the number of trunks needed for
 6 Erlangs of offered load? Answer 12
\layout Example*

For a blocking probability of 0.01, what is the number of trunks needed for
 18 Erlangs? Answer 28
\layout Example*

So load increase of 200 % only lead to a trunk increase of 133 %.
 Why? Let's look at the utilization factors.
 
\layout Example*


\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
\rho _{1} & = & \frac{6}{12}\left(1-0.01\right)=0.495\\
\rho _{2} & = & \frac{18}{28}\left(1-0.007\right)=0.6384
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset 

 The exact utilisation factors The 30 trunk system is thus more efficient.
